                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#definitions Definitions
          %h3#between_topological_spaces Between topological spaces
          %p
            While every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            sends
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+subsets"} compact subsets
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+subsets"} compact subsets
            (see at
            = succeed ")," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+images+of+compact+spaces+are+compact"} continuous images of compact spaces are compact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
            of a compact set along a continuous map is compact.
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            is a
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+map"} proper map
            if it is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+map"} closed
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            of any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+space"} compact
            subset is itself compact.
          %p
            A notion of proper homotopy between proper maps leads to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+homotopy+theory"} proper homotopy theory
          %p
            Similarly, one can consider the conditions on morphisms in other geometric situations, like
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
          %h3#between_schemes Between schemes
          %p
            A
            %strong
              proper morphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            is by definition a morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:X\to Y
            which is
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separated+morphism"}> separated
                ,
            %li
              %p
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+type+morphism"} finite type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universally+closed+morphism"} universally closed
                (the latter means that for every
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi h
                      %mo :
                      %mi Z
                      %mo →
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h: Z\to Y
                the pullback
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi h
                        %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %mi Z
                      %msub
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %mi X
                      %mo →
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} h^*(f): Z\times_Y X\to Z
                is closed).
          %p
            There is a classical and very practical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/valuative+criterion+of+properness"} valuative criterion of properness
            due Chevalley.
          %p
            We say that a scheme
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is
            %strong proper
            if the canonical map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Spec
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to \operatorname{Spec} \mathbb{Z}
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            is proper.
          %p
            Proper schemes are analogous to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+spaces"} compact topological spaces
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/base+change"} base change
            formulas for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            for proper and for smooth morphisms of schemes motivated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"} Grothendieck
            (in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pursuing+Stacks"} Pursuing Stacks
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibered+categories"} fibered categories
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Maltsiniotis"} Maltsiniotis
          %h3#between_locales Between locales
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locale"} locale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame"} frame
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O(L)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+of+opens"} frame of opens
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is given by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo ⊣
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⇄
                  %mi O
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi L
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^* \dashv f_* \colon O(K) \rightleftarrows O(L)
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
            function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^*
            preserves finitary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/meets"} meets
            (or equivalently is a frame
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homomorphism"} homomorphism
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/joins"} joins
          %p
            Such a map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is
            %strong proper
            iff the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+image"} direct image
            function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*
            preserves
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+joins"} directed joins
            (or equivalently is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott-continuous+function"} Scott-continuous
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preframes"} preframes
            = succeed "-like" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+reciprocity"} Frobenius reciprocity
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo ∪
                  %msup
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∪
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_*(U\cup f^*(V)) = f_*(U) \cup V
            (which by itself states that the map is
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+map"} closed
          %p
            In particular, the map
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mo →
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L\to 1
            is proper iff
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L
            is both
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+locale"} compact
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covert+space"} covert
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covert+space"} covert space
            for a proof.
          %p Proper maps of locales can also be characterized as those that are universally closed, i.e. every pullback of them (along any map of locales) is closed.
          %h3#between_toposes Between toposes
          %p
            Proper maps of locales can be generalized to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+morphisms"} geometric morphisms
            of
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+toposes"} Grothendieck toposes
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+geometric+morphism"} proper geometric morphism
          %p
            The topos-theoretic condition refers only to directed unions of subterminal objects, suggesting a stronger condition that it preserve all
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimits"} filtered colimits
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localic+toposes"} localic toposes
            = succeed "." do
              %em tidy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+toposes"} higher toposes
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (Inconsistent terminology)
              %br/
              In the world of 1-toposes, the concept of proper map splits into two subconcepts
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} tidy
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} proper
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{tidy} \Rightarrow \mathrm{proper}
            %p with the stronger concept being called “tidy”.
            %p
              More generally in
              = succeed "-toposes," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo −
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} proper
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo .
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %mi proper
                    %mo ⇒
                    %mn 0
                    %mo −
                    %mi proper
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\infty-\mathrm{proper} \Rightarrow ... \Rightarrow 1-proper \Rightarrow 0-proper
            %p
              This is where the inconsistency arises: in
              = succeed "-topos" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo :
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f\,:\, X \to Y
              will induce a proper map of 1-toposes but not a proper map of
              = succeed "-toposes" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %p
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+geometric+morphism"} proper geometric morphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+map"}> proper map
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+map"} open map
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+map"} closed map
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+morphism"}> open morphism
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+morphism"} closed morphism
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+base+change+theorem"} proper base change theorem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+homotopy+theory"} proper homotopy theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maps+from+compact+spaces+to+Hausdorff+spaces+are+closed+and+proper"} maps from compact spaces to Hausdorff spaces are closed and proper
          %h2#references References
          %ul#Maltsiniotis
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"}> Georges Maltsiniotis
              ,
